<robot name="tattotronix">
  <!--
    TATTOTRONIX: 5 DOF manipulator, ROS 2 Humble.

    Built to hold a tattoo pen, but nothing below the tool flange knows that.
    Swap the tool argument and the same arm carries a different end effector.

    Arguments
      hardware          gz | mock | pca9685 | none
                        'none' leaves ros2_control out entirely, which is what
                        display.launch.py wants: a description RViz can render
                        with joint_state_publisher_gui and no controller stack.
                        'pca9685' is the arm as built: its hobby servos driven
                        through a PCA9685 board by tattotronix_hardware.
      i2c_device        pca9685 only: the Linux I2C bus the board is on.
      i2c_address       pca9685 only: the board's address; 64 is 0x40, the
                        address with no jumpers soldered.
      dry_run           pca9685 only: true runs the whole stack with the board
                        simulated, sending nothing. For a first run, and for CI.
      servo_calibration pca9685 only: which channel drives each joint and how
                        its pulse maps to the angle. The default is declared,
                        not measured; see config/servo_calibration.yaml.
      tool              tattoo | none
                        The pen in tattoo_tool.xacro is primitives sized to a
                        catalogue rotary machine, not measured hardware. It is
                        the default because the TCP the tattoo paths are
                        planned for has to exist; 'none' strips it back to the
                        exported CAD when the question is about the arm.
      controllers_file  absolute path to the controller manager YAML, read by
                        the Gazebo plugin. Only consulted when hardware=gz.
      use_world_link    true bolts base_link to a world link, which is how the
                        arm is anchored in simulation. Set false when the
                        description is embedded in a larger cell.
      mass_model        printed (default) is the arm as built: each link's
                        printed shell, integrated from its mesh, plus the servos
                        mounted in it, generated into inertials_printed.xacro by
                        docs/scripts/mass_properties.py. box is the earlier
                        approximation by the solid box that fits each link's
                        bounding box, with masses written by hand - kept so the
                        two can be compared, and heavier than the arm by a factor
                        of about eight. Only inertia changes; geometry and
                        kinematics are identical in both.
  -->
  <!--
    Shared colours.

    Wrapped in a macro rather than declared at file scope on purpose. Both the
    arm and the tool macros include this file, and xacro does not deduplicate
    includes, so file-scope <material> elements would be emitted twice and the
    URDF parser rejects a duplicate material name. The macro is expanded once,
    by the top-level description.

    The arm is light grey because RViz lights a dark model badly: on the dark
    background the shading collapses and the whole arm reads as one silhouette
    with no edges. Grey keeps the links separable. The pen stays dark so it is
    obvious which part of the model is hardware and which is the placeholder.
  -->
  <!--
    Inertia helpers for the box mass model.

    The CAD behind the meshes does not ship mass properties, so this model
    approximates every link by the solid primitive that best matches its
    bounding box, with masses written by hand. It is no longer the default.
    Those masses implied densities of 7 to 14 g/cm^3 - denser than steel - and
    made the arm about eight times heavier than it is.

    The default is now mass_model:=printed: the volumes are integrated from
    the meshes themselves, and each link is its printed shell plus the servos
    mounted in it (docs/scripts/mass_properties.py, which writes
    inertials_printed.xacro). This file stays so the two can be compared.
  -->
  <!--
    Shared colours.

    Wrapped in a macro rather than declared at file scope on purpose. Both the
    arm and the tool macros include this file, and xacro does not deduplicate
    includes, so file-scope <material> elements would be emitted twice and the
    URDF parser rejects a duplicate material name. The macro is expanded once,
    by the top-level description.

    The arm is light grey because RViz lights a dark model badly: on the dark
    background the shading collapses and the whole arm reads as one silhouette
    with no edges. Grey keeps the links separable. The pen stays dark so it is
    obvious which part of the model is hardware and which is the placeholder.
  -->
  <!--
    TATTOTRONIX arm: 5 revolute joints plus a fixed tool flange.

    Kinematics carried over verbatim from the ROS 1 description
    (src/lesson_urdf/urdf/planar_3dof.xacro on branch main), with the joint
    origins converted from the CAD centimetre units to metres. The meshes are
    exported in their own joint frame, so every visual and collision sits at
    the link origin and only needs the 0.01 scale factor.

    Two things the ROS 1 file got away with and a ROS 2 control stack will not,
    both fixed here and called out so the change is not mistaken for drift:
      * every joint declared effort="0", which pins the actuator torque limit
        at zero and makes the arm unusable under any effort-aware controller;
      * the tool frame repeated the wrist joint offset and therefore landed
        outside the tool mount mesh.
  -->
  <!--
    Shared colours.

    Wrapped in a macro rather than declared at file scope on purpose. Both the
    arm and the tool macros include this file, and xacro does not deduplicate
    includes, so file-scope <material> elements would be emitted twice and the
    URDF parser rejects a duplicate material name. The macro is expanded once,
    by the top-level description.

    The arm is light grey because RViz lights a dark model badly: on the dark
    background the shading collapses and the whole arm reads as one silhouette
    with no edges. Grey keeps the links separable. The pen stays dark so it is
    obvious which part of the model is hardware and which is the placeholder.
  -->
  <!--
    Tattoo pen: a thin barrel along the tool axis with a conical tip, and the
    frame at that tip.

    Deliberately the simplest shape that can carry a TCP. The pen has not been
    built, so any detail here would be invention dressed up as hardware, and a
    detailed placeholder is worse than an obvious one: it invites people to
    measure off it. What this has to get right is the axis and the tip, because
    those are what tattoo paths are planned against, and both are replaceable
    by editing two numbers when the real pen is measured.

    The frame to plan in is tattoo_tcp, at the tip, +z pointing along the pen
    and into the work.
  -->
  <!--
    ros2_control hardware description.

    One <ros2_control> block serves every backend; only the plugin swaps.
      gz    -> gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem, driven by Gazebo Sim (Fortress)
      mock  -> mock_components/GenericSystem, for testing controllers and
               MoveIt with no simulator and no hardware attached
      pca9685 -> tattotronix_hardware/Pca9685ServoSystem, the arm as built:
               hobby servos driven through a PCA9685 board. Each joint gets
               its channel and calibration from servo_calibration.
    tattotronix.urdf.xacro rejects any other backend, so an unknown one fails
    at expansion rather than producing a description that controls nothing.

    The pca9685 joints have no effort state. A hobby servo reports nothing, so
    the driver refuses to invent one, and its position and velocity states are
    the command it last sent rather than a measurement.
  -->
  <!--
    Gazebo Sim (Fortress) specifics: the ros2_control system plugin and the
    per-link surface properties.

    Friction is deliberately high on the tool links. A tattoo pen that slides
    across the contact patch is worse than one that sticks, and the contact we
    care about is a needle held against skin, not a wheel.
  -->
  <!-- Printed structure. -->
  <material name="tattotronix_shell">
    <color rgba="0.78 0.79 0.81 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <!-- Brackets and fasteners, a shade down so joints read against links. -->
  <material name="tattotronix_steel">
    <color rgba="0.55 0.57 0.61 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <!-- Tool body. Deliberately the darkest thing in the scene. -->
  <material name="tattotronix_ink">
    <color rgba="0.13 0.13 0.16 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <!-- Needle, and anything else that has to be found at a glance. -->
  <material name="tattotronix_accent">
    <color rgba="0.85 0.25 0.20 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <link name="world"/>
  <!-- base -->
  <joint name="base_mount" type="fixed">
    <parent link="world"/>
    <child link="base_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="base_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/base_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_shell"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/base_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="-0.03125 -0.0013 0.02435"/>
      <mass value="1.5"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00142215625" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.0033948125000000003" iyz="0.0" izz="0.00422890625"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- joint 1: base yaw -->
  <joint name="joint_1" type="revolute">
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="shoulder_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.05"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
    <limit effort="20.0" lower="-1.57" upper="1.57" velocity="1.5"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="shoulder_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/shoulder_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_shell"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/shoulder_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.00075 0.0 0.03455"/>
      <mass value="0.9"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.0015074175" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.0010034355000000002" iyz="0.0" izz="0.0018435195"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- joint 2: shoulder pitch -->
  <joint name="joint_2" type="revolute">
    <parent link="shoulder_link"/>
    <child link="upper_arm_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.02866 -0.00364 0.0431385"/>
    <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
    <limit effort="20.0" lower="-1.57" upper="1.57" velocity="1.5"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="upper_arm_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/upper_arm_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_shell"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/upper_arm_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.0 -0.00255 0.0567"/>
      <mass value="0.6"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.0012645024999999998" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.001234292" iyz="0.0" izz="0.00018231050000000002"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- joint 3: elbow pitch -->
  <joint name="joint_3" type="revolute">
    <parent link="upper_arm_link"/>
    <child link="forearm_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0.0251 0.1201"/>
    <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
    <limit effort="20.0" lower="-1.57" upper="1.57" velocity="1.5"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="forearm_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/forearm_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_shell"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/forearm_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="-0.0109 -0.023 0.033"/>
      <mass value="0.45"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.0003898875" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.00047887499999999997" iyz="0.0" izz="0.0002476875"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- joint 4: wrist roll -->
  <joint name="joint_4" type="revolute">
    <parent link="forearm_link"/>
    <child link="wrist_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.0033 -0.02184 0.02885"/>
    <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
    <limit effort="20.0" lower="-1.57" upper="1.57" velocity="1.5"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="wrist_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/wrist_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_shell"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/wrist_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.08525 0.00915 -5e-05"/>
      <mass value="0.35"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00013307495833333332" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="0.0008865269583333335" iyz="0.0" izz="0.0009423125833333334"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- joint 5: wrist pitch -->
  <joint name="joint_5" type="revolute">
    <parent link="wrist_link"/>
    <child link="tool_mount_link"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.153 0.003 -0.002"/>
    <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
    <limit effort="20.0" lower="-1.57" upper="1.57" velocity="1.5"/>
    <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="tool_mount_link">
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/tool_mount_link.stl" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_steel"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/collision/tool_mount_link.dae" scale="0.01 0.01 0.01"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.0108 -0.0115 0.0"/>
      <mass value="0.15"/>
      <inertia ixx="2.0282625e-05" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="3.0265624999999995e-05" iyz="0.0" izz="2.3323249999999997e-05"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <!-- tool flange.
         tool0 is the frame every end effector attaches to, and its +z axis is
         the tool axis, so a tool mounts with no rotation of its own.

         The tool axis is the bracket's own +x, and the line it runs along is
         the middle of the three holes on the outer face, measured off the
         mesh:

           y = -12.064 mm, z = +5.489 mm

         The bracket has two sets of three holes and they are easy to confuse.
         The set bored along y, radius 1.50 mm at x = 0 and z = -8, 0 and
         +8 mm, goes through the 3 mm back plate: that is how the bracket bolts
         down, and it carries nothing. The set that matters is bored along x
         through the outer face, radius 1.50 mm, on a straight line at 8.00 mm
         pitch: (-5.203, +9.603), (-12.064, +5.489), (-18.925, +1.375).

         The middle one is the shaft. Behind it the face opens into a stepped
         boss of radius 2.50, 3.00 and 4.00 mm running back to x = 21.5 mm, all
         four cylinders concentric to within 5 um, which is a bearing seat and
         not a bolt hole. It also sits on the clamp mid plane, y = -12.06 mm,
         and on the midpoint of its two neighbours. Three independent readings
         of the same line.

         x = 29.0 mm puts the origin on that outer face, so the tool starts
         where the part ends.

         Read off the mesh, not off CAD datums. -->
  <joint name="tool_mount_to_tool0" type="fixed">
    <parent link="tool_mount_link"/>
    <child link="tool0"/>
    <origin rpy="0 1.5707963267948966 0" xyz="0.029 -0.012064 0.005489"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="tool0"/>
  <joint name="tool0_to_tattoo_pen" type="fixed">
    <parent link="tool0"/>
    <child link="tattoo_pen_link"/>
    <!-- No rotation: tool0 already defines +z as the tool axis. -->
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="tattoo_pen_link">
    <!-- Barrel: full length minus the taper. -->
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.0185"/>
      <geometry>
        <cylinder length="0.037" radius="0.003"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_ink"/>
    </visual>
    <!--
        Tip: a cone, because URDF has no cone primitive and a flat disc at the
        end of a needle reads wrong. The mesh is generated by
        docs/scripts/make_pen_tip.py and written in millimetres, hence the 0.001
        scale; the exported CAD meshes are in centimetres and carry 0.01
        instead. Its apex lands exactly on tattoo_tcp - the tip has not moved.
      -->
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.037"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://tattotronix_description/meshes/visual/tattoo_pen_tip.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="tattotronix_ink"/>
    </visual>
    <!--
        Collision stays the full cylinder. It is conservative, it is what a
        collision check should use, and it keeps the tip out of the contact
        solver where a sharp cone would only make it brittle.
      -->
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.0225"/>
      <geometry>
        <cylinder length="0.045" radius="0.003"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.0225"/>
      <mass value="0.020"/>
      <inertia ixx="3.42e-06" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="3.42e-06" iyz="0.0" izz="9e-08"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <joint name="tattoo_pen_to_tcp" type="fixed">
    <parent link="tattoo_pen_link"/>
    <child link="tattoo_tcp"/>
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.045"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="tattoo_tcp"/>
</robot>
